<h1>Assembly Line Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Assembly line conveyors transport workpieces through sequential stations where operators or robots perform assembly, fastening, inspection, and testing operations. These systems range from simple flat belt conveyors for manual assembly to precision pallet transfer systems with sub-millimeter positioning for robotic stations. Conveyor types include workstation belt conveyors, pallet transfer (workpiece carrier) systems, chain-on-edge conveyors, and <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyors</a>. The key design question is how much precision, reconfiguration flexibility, and workstation access the assembly process requires.</p>
<h2 id="types-heading">Assembly Conveyor Types</h2>
<h3>Workstation Belt Conveyors</h3>
<p>Flat belt conveyors run at slow, constant speed through a series of manual workstations. Operators stand or sit alongside the conveyor and perform assembly tasks as product moves past. VFD speed control adjusts belt speed to match operator pace and staffing levels. Belt width typically matches the workpiece width plus clearance.</p>
<p>Workstation conveyors integrate with ergonomic accessories: tilting work surfaces, tool balancers, parts bins, task lighting, and ESD (electrostatic discharge) grounding for electronics assembly. Small-frame belt conveyors suit most of this work. Dorner's <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/2200-series/2200-belted-conveyor" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2200 series</a>, for example, comes in belt widths from 1.75 to 24 inches and carries loads up to 120 lb.</p>
<h3>Pallet Transfer Systems</h3>
<p>Pallet transfer (workpiece carrier) systems carry products on machined pallets that circulate in a closed loop. Each pallet has precision fixtures, locating pins, nests, or clamps, that hold the workpiece in a repeatable position at every station. The conveyor transports pallets between stations; pop-up locating pins at each station register the pallet for robotic operations. Bosch Rexroth states its <a href="https://www.boschrexroth.com/en/pl/products/industrial-solutions/assembly-technology/conveyor-and-transfer-systems/ts-2plus-transfer-system/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TS 2plus transfer system</a> can reach a stop position with an accuracy of up to 0.015 mm using its TS 2Booster drive, without a separate positioning unit, and handles workpiece pallets from 160 x 160 mm to 1,200 x 1,200 mm with loads up to 240 kg.</p>
<p>Modular pallet transfer platforms serve electronics, medical device, automotive component, and consumer product assembly. Pallet transfer supports both manual and automated stations on the same line, operators load components at manual stations, and robots perform fastening or inspection at automated stations.</p>
<h3>Chain-on-Edge Conveyors</h3>
<p>Chain-on-edge conveyors use a precision chain running in a vertical plane (on its edge) with workpiece-carrying fixtures attached at regular intervals. Parts hang from or mount on fixtures that travel through assembly, painting, curing, and inspection stations. Common in <a href="/industries/automotive-conveyors/">automotive component assembly</a>, appliance manufacturing, and any operation where parts need 360-degree access for processing.</p>
<h3>Overhead Conveyors</h3>
<p>Overhead (power-and-free) conveyors carry product above the work floor on trolleys suspended from an overhead track. The power chain runs continuously; individual trolleys can stop at stations while others continue moving. Overhead conveyors maximize floor space for operators and equipment. Used in appliance assembly, vehicle assembly, and heavy equipment production.</p>
<h2 id="motion-heading">Continuous vs. Indexing Motion</h2>
<h3>Continuous Motion</h3>
<p>Continuous assembly conveyors run at constant speed. Operators perform tasks while the product moves. The station length and belt speed determine the available work time per station: work time (seconds) = station length (feet) / belt speed (FPM) × 60. A 6-foot station at 10 FPM gives each operator 36 seconds.</p>
<p>Continuous motion suits large products (vehicles, appliances, furniture) where the work content per station is long and operators walk alongside the product. It also suits high-volume, simple operations (labeling, stickering, visual inspection) where operators need only seconds per piece.</p>
<h3>Indexing Motion</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing assembly conveyors</a> advance product one station pitch, stop, and hold during the dwell time while stations operate. All stations operate simultaneously during the dwell, then all advance together. Cycle time = index time + dwell time. Throughput = 60 / cycle time.</p>
<p>Indexing suits small-to-medium products requiring precise positioning for automated operations (robotic assembly, screw driving, press fitting, vision inspection). Servo-driven indexing provides recipe-based pitch changes for different product sizes. The slowest station determines the dwell time, and therefore the line's maximum throughput.</p>
<h2 id="ergo-heading">Ergonomic Conveyor Design</h2>
<p>Assembly conveyor ergonomics directly affect operator productivity, quality, and injury rates. Key ergonomic parameters:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Working height:</strong> Set from the operator's elbow height and the task. <a href="https://www.ccohs.ca/oshanswers/ergonomics/standing/standing_basic.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CCOHS recommends</a> a work surface about 5 cm above elbow height for precision work such as electronic assembly, about 5 to 10 cm below elbow height for light assembly-line work, and 20 to 40 cm below for heavy work. Adjustable-height conveyor supports accommodate different operators and products.</li>
<li><strong>Reach distance:</strong> Keep parts bins and tools within easy forearm reach of the operator's normal working position. Size conveyor width so the far edge does not force a full-arm reach or leaning.</li>
<li><strong>Lighting:</strong> Precision and inspection tasks need more light than general assembly. Set illuminance for the task and add task lights on the conveyor frame where general lighting falls short.</li>
<li><strong>Anti-fatigue:</strong> Anti-fatigue mats at standing stations. Ergonomic chairs at seated stations. Conveyor belt surface at a height that minimizes shoulder elevation and wrist deviation.</li>
<li><strong>ESD protection:</strong> For electronics assembly, the conveyor belt, frame, and work surfaces must dissipate static electricity. ESD-safe belt materials, grounded frames, and operator wrist straps prevent electrostatic damage to sensitive components.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a comprehensive view of how conveyors connect with <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated systems</a>, PLCs, and Industry 4.0 data collection, see our automation guide. For cost considerations, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
