<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is an assembly line conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>An assembly line conveyor transports workpieces through sequential stations for assembly, fastening, inspection, and testing. Runs at slow controlled speed for manual stations or indexes between stations for automated operations.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is a pallet transfer system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Carries workpieces on machined pallets with precision fixtures. Circulates in a loop through assembly stations. Locating pins or positioning units register pallets with sub-millimeter repeatability for robotic operations.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Continuous vs. indexing assembly conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Continuous: constant speed, operators work while product moves. Suits large products. <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">Indexing</a>: advance-stop-dwell cycles, precise positioning. Suits automated stations. The slowest station sets the line throughput.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">What speed for manual assembly conveyors?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Set speed from work content: station length divided by work time per station. VFD speed control adjusts for different products or staffing. Set working height from elbow height: CCOHS recommends about 5 to 10 cm below elbow height for light assembly and about 5 cm above for precision work.</p>

</div>
